# TLS setup builds OpenSSL contexts on pool threads; the system libcrypto's own locking is not instrumented, so TSan
# sees its accesses as racy. Only stacks through these two functions are suppressed. The system trust store's X509
# objects (vm/tls_trust.c) are shared read-only by every context; if TSan reports them in handshake stacks, that is
# the same uninstrumented locking (their extension cache is filled under the X509 lock), not a VM race.
race:agc_tls_client_config
race:agc_tls_server_config
# A forked child sets `environ` to the child's environment before exec (libuv's uv__process_child_init). The child has
# only the forking thread, but TSan keeps the parent's history: a scheduler that read `environ` before the fork (the
# environment copy of the next Process.spawn) looks like a racing thread. Only that function's stack is suppressed.
race:uv__process_child_init
# libuv's signal handler (uv__signal_handler) walks the process-wide watcher tree under libuv's signal lock, which is a
# pipe (a read takes it, a write gives it back). TSan does not see that lock taken inside an asynchronous handler, so a
# SIGCHLD that lands on a scheduler thread looks like a race with the uv_signal_start of the first Process.spawn on the
# I/O thread. A plain libuv program reports the same pair. Only that handler's stack is suppressed.
race:uv__signal_handler
# EVP digests, HMACs, ciphers, CMACs, KDFs and RAND_bytes look up and fill OpenSSL's fetch caches (methods,
# providers, generators) under the system libcrypto's own locks and atomics, which TSan cannot see: when two
# schedulers hash, sign, encrypt, derive or draw at once, it reports a race on that cached data (#532). TSan unwinds by
# frame pointers, which libcrypto does not keep, so a report skips the adapter function that calls libcrypto
# (agc_digest, agc_hmac, agc_secure_random, agc_des_ede3_cbc, agc_cmac, agc_aes_256_gcm, agc_pbkdf2_hmac_sha256,
# agc_scrypt) and shows its caller first. Only the seven out-of-line wrappers in vm/crypto.c that do nothing but call
# the adapter are named, so a race in the builtin code around them is still reported.
race:digest_compute
race:hmac_compute
race:secure_draw
race:cipher_compute
race:cmac_compute
race:gcm_compute
race:kdf_compute
